      "git_blob_sha": "7882f107da5a48b9db03209f0442cc46bf0ec6ce",
      "stable_id": "R-HSA-381026",
      "display_name": "ATF6 (ATF6-alpha) translocates from the cytosol to the nucleus",
      "species": "Homo sapiens",
      "summary": "The cytosolic N-terminal cleavage product of ATF6-alpha transits to the nucleus."
    },
    {
      "canonical_path": "reactome/R-HSA-381135.md",
      "archive_member": "gene_candidates/ATF6/reactome/R-HSA-381135.md",
      "staged_path": "tmp/seed22-stage/quarantine/auxiliary/gene_candidates/ATF6/reactome/R-HSA-381135.md",
      "kind": "reactome",
      "bytes": 789,
      "sha256": "e9e0440084475b8be956b62e00fc83cd0af6153fa08c65992c7333b7aa0084a4",
      "git_blob_sha": "e3e2cce975afab0d1711706b3ded1c5cb19fb926",
      "stable_id": "R-HSA-381135",
      "display_name": "MBTPS1 (S1P) cleaves ATF6 (ATF6-alpha)",
      "species": "Homo sapiens",
      "summary": "Once in the Golgi, ATF6-alpha undergoes two sequential proteolytic cleavages. S1P catalyzes the first of these, probably cleaving the ATF6-alpha polypeptide between residues 418 and 419 based on homology with known S1P cleavage sites in other proteins."
    },
    {
      "canonical_path": "reactome/R-HSA-381158.md",
      "archive_member": "gene_candidates/ATF6/reactome/R-HSA-381158.md",
      "staged_path": "tmp/seed22-stage/quarantine/auxiliary/gene_candidates/ATF6/reactome/R-HSA-381158.md",
      "kind": "reactome",
      "bytes": 2003,
      "sha256": "0c6c2786c63273361bb8a9d27710eeabb8c91ecb9c147c1748a26d740e06f616",
      "git_blob_sha": "39006120f7f2aca18368ec231248c3f6bd7bd1a9",
      "stable_id": "R-HSA-381158",
      "display_name": "ATF6:HSPA5 (ATF6-alpha:BiP) dissociates and HSPA5 binds unfolded protein",
      "species": "Homo sapiens",
      "summary": "ATF6-alpha is a transmembrane protein located in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) membrane with N-terminal cytoplasmic and C-terminal luminal domains. BiP binds the luminal domain of ATF6-alpha via the substrate binding domain of BiP. Binding of BiP blocks 2 Golgi localization sequences on ATF6-alpha, maintaining ATF6-alpha in the ER. <br> BiP is also a general chaperone capable of binding unfolded proteins in the ER lumen. When chaperone activity in the ER is overwhelmed, BiP dissociates from ATF6-alpha and binds the excess unfolded proteins. It is unclear whether the dissociation is due to competition of unfolded proteins for BiP or to a more specific interaction between BiP and ATF6-alpha. The dissociation exposes the Golgi localization sequences of ATF6-alpha and allows ATF6-alpha to transit to the Golgi."
    },
    {
      "canonical_path": "reactome/R-HSA-381186.md",
      "archive_member": "gene_candidates/ATF6/reactome/R-HSA-381186.md",
      "staged_path": "tmp/seed22-stage/quarantine/auxiliary/gene_candidates/ATF6/reactome/R-HSA-381186.md",
      "kind": "reactome",
      "bytes": 881,
      "sha256": "5429964a62ddcdb54566ea4205511f1fe38e2682bfc8a8516f58042936aa78f6",
      "git_blob_sha": "3ecc3dc37061f14044dd7a0fb067b7a33d7c50fa",
      "stable_id": "R-HSA-381186",
      "display_name": "ATF6 (ATF6-alpha) translocates from the endoplasmic reticulum to the Golgi",
      "species": "Homo sapiens",
      "summary": "The association between ATF6-alpha and BiP causes ATF6-alpha to be retained in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER). Once dissociated from BiP, the two Golgi Localization Sequences on ATF6-alpha are exposed and ATF6-alpha transits from the ER to the Golgi Apparatus."
    },
    {
